# Help people find British Sign Language content

Offer British Sign Language (BSL) content only when a verified signed translation exists. BSL is a language, not an icon style or hand gestures added to an animation. These examples are local guidance. They do not add a Home Assistant screen or perform a device action.

## No translation is bundled

The current Civica theme has no verified BSL translation of its help. The examples therefore show availability, editorial preparation and failure states. They contain no player, synthetic signer, thumbnail or non-functional “Watch” link.

<%= example('nhs-bsl-discovery') %>

## When a translation is commissioned

Agree the content and terminology with a qualified translation provider and BSL users. Translate the real household task, including supported device states and failure messages, and check it against the current written version. Record the owner, permissions, language, duration, review date and source version.

Then link with the task and the duration, for example “Watch the British Sign Language guide to changing a theme (2 minutes)”. Put the link beside the related instructions and in any language or access-format navigation, not only in a footer. A BSL icon is optional and supplementary. It needs a known source and testing, and it must not imply endorsement by the NHS or any other body.

<%= example('nhs-bsl-content-bundle') %>

## Players and other formats

Use an accessible player with keyboard controls, visible focus, pause and full-screen support. Do not autoplay. Keep the signer large enough to follow and check that captions do not cover the signing. Transcripts, captions, audio description and BSL meet different needs, so label each format by what it is.

If the signed content becomes unavailable, keep the written instructions and say what failed. Link to a translation only if its destination is still verified. Do not replace BSL with an unrelated help video.

<%= example('nhs-bsl-link-unavailable') %>

## Keep the boundary clear

The theme can show a link, a player and an availability state. It cannot supply a translation. Do not infer a language preference from disability or from one interaction. Store a preference only when the product supports it, and say what it covers. Written help must stay available when remote media is blocked, offline or waiting for consent.

## Review with users

Test discovery, comprehension, signer visibility, zoom on a phone and the controls with BSL users. When the written guide changes, review the signed guide too, so a stale translation does not stay online. Do not claim BSL availability until both the content and its link have passed review. Use the [Civica accessibility checks](../accessibility/testing.html) for the page.
